// ---- Stage 9
doc.push(H2("Stage 9 — chat-5: an audited ledger, an ingest path, three supply-chain tools, and attribution (2026-11-11)"));
doc.push(P("Chat-5 opened with an instruction that set its shape for the whole session: work the open items from low priority to high, one at a time, then wrap everything and hand off. The numbered list in CURRENT-STATE is priority-ordered rather than effort-ordered — a point the owner had raised at chat-4's close — and this session is the demonstration, because the second item on the list was the largest piece of work in it. Five tasks closed."));
doc.push(P("The first was not code at all. D07 §14.5 has carried an open venue question since the docket was drafted, and the session's first deliverable was a counsel brief and a Phase 0 formation checklist written so that a lawyer is answering a specific question rather than being handed the problem. The venue answer stays open and labelled open, because it is a counsel decision and guessing at it in a customer document would be the exact failure this project keeps finding in other places."));

doc.push(H3("9.1 — The ledger audit, and a false claim in a customer document"));
doc.push(P("A fresh agent was given the disposition ledger, the export bundle and the replication series, with the instruction to falsify them rather than review them. It found four defects, one critical: a rewritten chain verified against a bundle already held, because the previous-bundle comparison checked the slice it was handed rather than the slice it should have been. Two more were invisible for structural reasons — a mid-chain deletion that the whole-series walk never saw, and `INSERT OR REPLACE` defeating the append-only triggers on the disposition table because SQLite does not fire a `BEFORE DELETE` trigger for it unless recursive triggers are on. The fourth was duplicate JSON keys, which two parsers can read as two different records."));
doc.push(callout(
  "One of the four was a customer-facing claim the code did not back",
  "B01 §7 item 8 asserted a property the export did not have. That is the category of defect that matters most in this project, and not because it is technically the worst: a customer could check it against us and be right to complain. Code is verifiable by running it. Prose is not, and prose that overstates a control is worse than prose that undersells one, because the overstatement is what a regulator reads.",
  { fill: "FBEAEA", labelColor: RED },
));

doc.push(H3("9.2 — The ingest path: narrowing the largest gap, not closing it"));
doc.push(P("`POST /api/ingest` now applies the three-gate chain B02 §2 has specified since the flow map was drawn — schema, then timestamp, then plausibility, in that order and for stated reasons — accepting for scoring, retaining as suspect without scoring, or refusing with a named reason and logging the refusal. Twenty-four tests. This is the item D00 calls the largest gap in the register, and the record is careful about the size of the claim: the schema is a demonstration schema, the endpoint has no authentication, and no accepted reading is scored. **Building the validator did not put a Railroad reading behind any number**, and both the build record and the gap register say that in those words rather than letting 'ingest path built' imply otherwise."));

doc.push(H3("9.3 — The supply chain, and a distinction that had been collapsing"));
doc.push(PB("D09 §4.4 disclosed three supply-chain gaps: SBOM, hash verification of dependency downloads, and GPG-signed releases. Three tools were built for them — `tools/sbom.py`, `tools/pin_requirements.py`, `tools/sign_release.py` — and none of them is applied. That distinction is the point of the stage. The project had been using one word for two states, and 'implemented' had drifted to mean 'a tool exists'. The three-state discipline this pass adopted — **absent, built, applied** — is now carried per control in D09 §4.4, because a disclosed gap that is built-but-not-applied is still a gap and a reader has to be able to see which one they are looking at."));
doc.push(P("There was a mistake worth recording in the middle of it: the pinning tool's first version pinned the hashes it had just downloaded, which records whatever the server served rather than what was verified. A pinning mechanism that trusts its own fetch is not a pinning mechanism. It was found by asking what the tool would do against a compromised mirror, not by a test."));

doc.push(H3("9.4 — Attribution: what it closed, and the defect the audit was for"));
doc.push(P("The owner delegated the design question — asked which attribution claim the Platform should be able to make, they answered that they were unsure and trusted the judgement. The answer built here is per-reviewer HMAC keys, an append-only hash-chained registry, and attestations that travel **inside each exported record**. The placement is a decision worth recording: a top-level attribution block would have failed the `bundle_no_extras` check added at chat-4's close, and loosening that check would have traded a security property for layout. Inside `records`, the block is covered by the bundle signature automatically, and `row_hash()` ignores it because it selects a named field list, so no existing `record_hash` changed."));
doc.push(PB("The scheme is symmetric, and that was a deliberate choice over Ed25519. There is no vetted curve implementation in this deployment's dependency set, and hand-rolling one in a product whose pitch includes not hand-rolling crypto would be a worse gap than a symmetric attestation whose limits are written down. Those limits are stated in the module, in D09 §4.5.5 and in the build record: the claim is *a registered key was used*, not *a person signed*, and custody is what binds one to the other. The registry itself is **hash-chained, not signed** — tamper-evident to a reader who recomputes the chain, which is weaker than authentication, and several documents had been calling it signed until this pass corrected them."));
doc.push(callout(
  "The finding that justified the audit on its own",
  "The `reviewer` label inside an exported attestation was not covered by the HMAC. The signed message is a fixed prefix plus the row's `record_hash` — the reviewer name is not in it, so the label was decorative. A holder of **any** registered reviewer key could take a row naming somebody else, compute a perfectly valid attestation with their own key, write the other reviewer's name into the block, re-sign the bundle with the export key, and **both verifiers returned a pass**. Every test in a suite of forty-five passed anyway, because the suite was written by the person who believed the label was covered. The fix requires the verifier be told which reviewer each key belongs to; hashing the name into the message was rejected because it makes the attestation non-transferable and moves the same problem one step later.",
  { fill: "FBEAEA", labelColor: RED },
));
doc.push(P("Eight more findings came with it: ordering checks guarded by truthiness, so deleting a field disabled the check; a check-then-write race in `attest()` between reading the registry and writing the attestation; `attest` and the export disagreeing about which registry entry is the registration after a certificate renewal, so an honest bundle failed at the recipient; malformed blocks raising `AttributeError` instead of a named failure; a docstring claiming a registry signature that does not exist; a standalone-verifier branch no test reached; and a revocation that could be undone by re-registering the same key bytes, which the mutation run exposed as dead duplicate code in the wrong function. All nine are §4 of the build record and each is now a test."));
doc.push(P("Verification for the stage: forty mutations against the three modules, forty killed, zero survived — ten of them added specifically to cover what the audit found. The suite went from 180 to 238."));

doc.push(H3("9.5 — The lesson this stage adds"));
doc.push(P("Three of the five tasks in this session ended with an audit finding something the author's own tests could not, and every session in this project has now had that happen. The pattern is not carelessness. It is that the person who writes the claim also writes the test that agrees with it, and no amount of care inside that loop breaks the loop. The standing rule — a fresh agent told to falsify — is what has been doing the work, and Stage 9's head-line finding is the clearest case yet, because it survived a green suite that had been specifically designed to attack the thing it was in."));
doc.push(PB("The second lesson is a document one, and it accumulated rather than arrived: most of this session's corrections were to prose, not code. The registry was repeatedly described as 'signed' when it is hash-chained; a customer document claimed a property the export did not have; the rate card priced a registry as unbuilt that is now partly built. None of these would fail a test."));

